title: ClickFix - Browser Process Spawning Command or Script Interpreter
id: e68b0f4a-8fba-4abd-bef6-bd22bf57b472
status: experimental
description: >
    Detects the ClickFix execution pattern Huntress documented: a browser page
    talks the victim through pasting a command into the Windows Run box or a
    PowerShell window, so the resulting interpreter is a child of the browser
    process rather than of Explorer, a mail client or a management agent. The
    newer chains built on this entry point add lateral movement, payloads
    hidden inside PNG files and Node.js remote access trojans, none of which
    need a software vulnerability. Lineage is the only durable signal because
    Huntress published no hashes or domains for these chains.
references:
    - https://www.huntress.com/blog/the-evolving-tradecraft-of-clickfix
    - https://secquill.com/articles/huntress-details-how-clickfix-scams-have-grown-beyond-simple-copy-and-paste-tricks/
author: Tia N. List
date: 2026/11/12
tags:
    - attack.t1204
    - attack.t1059
logsource:
    category: process_creation
    product: windows
detection:
    selection_parent_browser:
        ParentImage|endswith:
            - '\chrome.exe'
            - '\msedge.exe'
            - '\firefox.exe'
            - '\brave.exe'
            - '\opera.exe'
            - '\vivaldi.exe'
    selection_child_interpreter:
        Image|endswith:
            - '\cmd.exe'
            - '\powershell.exe'
            - '\pwsh.exe'
            - '\mshta.exe'
            - '\rundll32.exe'
            - '\wscript.exe'
            - '\cscript.exe'
            - '\msiexec.exe'
    condition: selection_parent_browser and selection_child_interpreter
falsepositives:
    - Browser-integrated installers and 'open with' flows that hand a download to msiexec.exe or rundll32.exe
    - Enterprise launchers and kiosk shells that start cmd.exe or PowerShell from a browser-hosted application
    - Remote-support tools whose client runs inside a browser tab and legitimately starts a helper process
level: medium
